Z||000000||Fleabitdog||05-23-2001||09:08 AM||||Can anybody recomend a good isp. I have been with AOHE!! for over 5 years and thier service just gets worse. I need a good isp with good mail service. I have tried MSN, Prodigy,Sprynet,Compuserve,and AT&T. I would like to try something different, but would appreciate any feedback on the ones I have tried(it has been about three years since I have tried anything but aol) I am looking forward to telling aol to stick it where the sun don't shine. Thanks  Dave||||reg||13
Z||000001||FieroJoe||05-23-2001||09:28 AM||||I've never used AOL.  I've always been signed up with a local ISP.  Check in your phonebook, i bet that it is full of ISP's in your area.  We pay $15.99/mo for unlimited service, and it isnt especially fast, but it is reliable.  We only have 56k anyways.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/joesig_1.jpg"> <BR>'87 Coupe 5spd.<BR>AIM:FieroJoe87||||reg||1
Z||000002||Raydar||05-23-2001||09:43 AM||||Check out Earthlink, if they serve your area. <A HREF="http://www.earthlink.net" TARGET=_blank>www.earthlink.net</A> <BR>They recently purchased my old ISP (Mindspring), but their customer service is still first rate. They have several different plans. They also have an integrated software package that includes a pile of stuff. (I don't use the SW, though. It's not compatible with NT. I just set up my own connection.) I also have DSL through them. From the time I placed my DSL order until the time I was "on the air" was less than two weeks.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"> <BR>Of course, much of that depends upon the local telephone company, but I'm very happy with Earthlink.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>88 black Formula<BR>88 Duke coupe <BR>88 "wadded up" Formula (parts car)||||reg||14
Z||000003||Songman||05-23-2001||10:22 AM||||I used Earthlink before I got my cable modem. They are hard to beat. That is who I would recommend. <P>I had AOL for about 6 months way back years ago when I first got online. Here's my thoughts on AOL. They are good for people who are just learning about the internet. BUT... some people think AOL is the internet and never go on to find out what is really out there...<P>I'll end up with another AOL account someday for one reason... I travel and you can dial-up to AOL from almost anywhere. I miss too muc Fiero stuff when I am traveling now!<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Fleabitdog||05-23-2001||11:49 AM||||Songman you can get on the byo isp for about 10.00 mor a month( come to think of it aol rates are going up soon) I used the internet alot but I also enjoyed the other things you could do on aol. But I am pretty fed up with crappy access numbers( Sometimes it take 30 minutes or more to get online and that is not because the # is busy all the time) and then aol freezes( I have to unplug my laptop and pull the battery and then go through the scan when I reboot my computer.) and sometimes the connection just disconnects. Plus all the graphics from aol advertisers. It is pretty frustrating. and also if you are idle for 5 minutes or so you get a pop up box that says you have been idle and do you wish to stay online?  Heck yea with all the work to get online I don't think they should ask that stupid question. Then if you don't click yes soon, Bamn!!! You are disconnected. Aol used to be my favorite but not any more.||||reg||1
Z||000005||mblackwell||05-23-2001||12:07 PM||||Big ISPs suck, and AOL is the absolute worst.  I wouldn't use AOL if they paid me (unless it was like $100 a day).  Find a local ISP with about ~200 customers, it will be much better.  THe worse part of AOL is thier horrible email system.  It is totally screwed up and not fully compatable with REAL email.  A real ISP won't give you any software, you can use whatever mail program you like.  I use Outlook 2000 myself.<P><p>[This message has been edited by mblackwell (edited 05-23-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||Oreif||05-23-2001||12:10 PM||||Earthlink works great. I am now on a cable system (AT&T use to be TCI). Songman, If you want access to the net while away, Just call your provider and ask them for a dial-up phone number. My cable provider supplies one if you tell them you travel alot. They give you the big speech that it isn't as fast as your cable connection (DUH) But you can access you account and the net from any phone.||||reg||1
Z||000007||KnightRyder31||05-23-2001||12:25 PM||||Just get a cable modem.<BR>I would, if I could afford it.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000008||Raydar||05-23-2001||01:24 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by mblackwell:<BR><B>A real ISP won't give you any software, you can use whatever mail program you like.  I use Outlook 2000 myself.<P><BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Earthlink's software is provided as a convenience. It's not mandatory to use it, like AOL. They just have some useful stuff bundled together. Outlook Express, IE, Netscape, an IRC client, and some other stuff. They used to include Eudora Light. Not sure, now.<BR>I didn't use any of it when I set up my 'puter. IE5/Outlook Express 5 came with Win2K, and I just set up my own dialup connection. DL'd winpoet for DSL.<BR>And yes, I know Outlook Express has its shortcomings, but it works okay, and it's free with the OS. <P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>88 black Formula<BR>88 Duke coupe <BR>88 "wadded up" Formula (parts car)||||reg||1
Z||000009||Fiero5||05-23-2001||01:33 PM||||That would be AOL/Time Warner now.<P>And be carefull using programs like Outlook, as many of these will automatically open/load attachements. If the e-mail has a virus in it, you got it. If it has a command to wipe your hard drive out, it will launch the command.<P>Steve<P>------------------<BR>                     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fiero1sig.JPG">                   <BR>http://www.kelleyfamilyfieros.homestead.com/index.html||||reg||12
Z||000010||Fleabitdog||05-23-2001||01:54 PM||||Well, I just signed up for Earthlink, now Have to wait an hour and call cust/svc. to get it set up on my laptop. I used to really like aol, but anymore it seems like there are more problems and they are more concerned with signing new members than taking care of thier old members by fixing thier(own aol) problems. Thanks for all the help. Dave||||reg||1
Z||000011||thomas_l||05-23-2001||02:51 PM||||OK - just half a thumbs up  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> I am in the ISP business and one thing I can say about AOL - you can install it on just about anything and make it work. AOL will work despite itself. I think if you installed AOL on a toaster or lawnmower it would have the right drivers. Now then... not getting busy signals is another issue. If you are having connectivity (i.e. you get through but cannot connect), especially with different ISP's, that is probably a function of the crappy software driven modems most PC's come with in the last 3 years. PCMCIA modems on laptops are pretty safe, USRobotics/3com sportster, Zoom, and one of the Soundblaster models <I>NON-Winmodems</I> work much better than others. If you are paying less than $60 for a modem you are getting a crappy software driven device. I have a &lt;insert my employers name here&gt; top of the line laptop and it has a wheezy no-good software modem. But the 100MB NIC goes well with my cable modem  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||10
Z||000012||My7Fieros||05-23-2001||04:03 PM||||My only real complaint w/ AOL is they are raising their prices to $23.90 a month starting in July. Other than that, its all I use. I guess its all what you're used to.<BR>Somebody said something about their bad emails....what!?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"> I have to strongly disagree. AOL has the absolute EASIEST and efficient emails...EVER. Outlook express is nothing but pure complicated CRAP! Not only that, but lots of email providers (like hotmail, webtv, prodigy mail link, ect.) have a limit on how much email it can store. Nothing like sending somebody an email only to get it back due to "full mailbox"...PUH LEEZ! AOL dosnt have this problem.<BR>AOL is the biggest internet provider for a reason...its easy to use. <BR>So dispite the fact that they're raising their prices (first time in 3 years), Im gonna stick with em. I like the IMs, buddy list, and basically the way AOL works. <BR>Ok, Ive said my peace now....<BR>(AOL you can send me the check now!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> )M7F||||reg||1
Z||000013||JSocha||05-23-2001||04:21 PM||||My7:  I'm glad your staying on and liking AOL.  LOL -&gt;I own stock in them which has made me money!  That is about as close to AOL I will ever want to get  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jsocha_2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000014||Jefrysuko||05-23-2001||05:31 PM||||I just got a notice from aol that their unlimited service is going up from ~$21 to ~$23.  I don't remember the exact amounts.  I am a student and my main computer usage is done at the college (T3-Line).  Once that ends I will be loosing AOL at home for cable or dsl and not looking back.  It is a joke trying to pull up a long thread with pictures.||||reg||1
Z||000015||KRMFiero||05-23-2001||07:37 PM||||My favorite has always been Mindspring/Earthlink.. i use aol i hate it it always crashes and everything, slow... I'm just waiting for Cox @ home and then im switching to that  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  damn hick town dowesnt offer it yet :'(<P>Kyle||||reg||1
Z||000016||Songman||05-23-2001||08:42 PM||||Orief,<P>I have looked at what @Home offers for dial-up. I wouldn't mind doing it since I would still have my same e-mail and all of that. And I could even deal with dial-up speeds while traveling, but they want to charge me by the minute for dial-up when I am away from home. The worst part of that is that they don't even publish what that per minute rate is.Other cable ISPs may not do this, but mine sure does....<P>For that, I'd just as soon suffer through AOL till something better comes along. Maybe a portable two way satellite link...<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000017||LZeitgeist||05-24-2001||07:57 AM||||I've been using AOL for 6 years now, and except for that few-month period of time a few years ago before they expanded their systems to accomodate the number of subscribers they had, I've never had problems with them. <P>I went from a 49K connection in a relatively new house to a 26K connection in an older apartment, and I *still* like the service. Yes, once in a blue moon I might lose my connection and have to sign back on again (this happens *maybe* once a week, if that), but seeing as I would much rather have a dial-up connection than a full-time link (no matter *what* ISP I choose), I would most likely be booted at those times no matter what ISP I was using. <P>Seems to me that 90% of the people who bash AOL are the ones who have never used it... it doesn't seem like that in this particular thread (which is a nice change), but it seems like the most vocal opponents to AOL are the ones who say "I would NEVER use that crappy service!!!" - if they've never used it, then how would they know? <P>Second-hand opinions mean next to nothing to me... most of them are just blowing hot air just to have something to say... gotta take it all with a grain of salt and ultimately make your own choice anyway.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000018||Songman||05-24-2001||09:04 AM||||Well, I'm sure glad I started my comment by saying that I used to use it.. that qualified my statement that it is crappy service and that people think AOL IS the internet when AOL is in fact just a tiny corner of the internet. I still use AOL when I connect from the studio and I still hate it just as bad. I connect from there with AOL, minmize it, and then connect to the real internet with a real browser.<P>Funny how every opinion that anyone gives though, someone has to make it a personal thing instead of just letting people have their opinions about the subject in question... Oh well... I like everybody so I ain't gonna take anything that is said personally.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000019||LZeitgeist||05-24-2001||09:31 AM||||I'm not sure if you were referring to me directly or just speaking in general, but I just wanted to say that I wasn't taking anything personally... just reporting my personal experiences with it.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>However, if I may clarify a couple points mentioned...<BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Well, I'm sure glad I started my comment by saying that I used to use it.. that qualified my statement that it is crappy service and that people think AOL IS the internet when AOL is in fact just a tiny corner of the internet.<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>No, it just qualified your statement that *you* don't care for it. It works just fine for me, and for countless numbers of people I am in weekly contact with. Just because it didn't ring your bell doesn't make it a "crappy service".  <P>And also, I keep hearing this 'tiny corner of the Internet' comment... yes, AOL has a great deal of 'stuff' inside it's programs, but I can type and reach any URL I choose to, just like I could if I was on Earthlink, RoadRunner, etc. etc. - I can access just as much of the Internet as you can on your ISP, whatever it happens to be. A difference which makes no difference *is* no difference.<P>Also, (quoting myself)<BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Seems to me that 90% of the people who bash AOL are the ones who have never used it... it doesn't seem like that in this particular thread (which is a nice change),<HR></BLOCKQUOTE> <BR>I just wanted to reiterate that this thread seems to be a good one insofar as there are actually people who have *used* AOL discussing things in this thread, and it's nice to hear informed opinions on the subject instead of the usual clueless and reflex-driven bashers.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Hey, as long as we can get *here*, what else *is* there?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000020||Songman||05-24-2001||09:34 AM||||I can agree with that!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/icons/icon14.gif"> <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||10
Z||000021||thomas_l||05-24-2001||10:37 AM||||I think what some folks don't see behind the slick user interface is that until about 6-7 years ago, AOL was in fact a giant BBS with unique features and content not publicly available and only included limited Internet access (e-mail and news). As the web took off, they have bent to market pressure and morphed into an ISP that offers somewhat unique features and content that nowadays is easily duplicated by others (i.e. Instant Messenger). It's really just a portal service on steroids now. Their value is a load & run service that runs on nearly any home computer just about anywhere. Back in '94 when I got re-interested in computers, you could pay the rare ISP $20/month for dial-up access and get the e-mail/browser/etc free but you had to figure out a lot yourself or you could buy AOL for $20 + 6¢/min and have something that just worked and had everything in one package. Big downloads were out of the question with a 2400 modem so you used sneakernet alot. There were very very few people patient and knowledgeable enough to just load up a terminal program (remember ProComm? ASCI graphics? constant redials because your favorite BBS only had 4 lines in?) and find BBS's to dial into. For free in most cases. Same thing today - either get hooked up with free stuff for $20 or kick in a few more bucks per month for ease of installation/use and some semi-unique content. For that matter, share all your marketing secrets and some monitor space for ad banners and get free dial-up too.<P>Personally, I haven't really used AOL for years but my wife is definitely not a power user and uses AOL frequently. Since I have had high speed Internet since '95 (ISDN then cable when it became available) we keep a ISP only (bring your own access option) account going with AOL for $10/mo. She's happy with her easy to use connection and keeps up with her family who are also using AOL for the most part. And there's nothing more important than the boss being happy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||14
Z||000022||sjp777||05-24-2001||09:12 PM||||AT&T cable broadband rocks. They had a lot of problems when it first rolled out, but the last couple of months the speed is kickin butt. I tested it the other night and was getting better than a 1 meg download speed.<P>$40 a month, but you get what you pay for.<P>Just don't let the installer load the @Home software. I just run IE6 for my browser and Outlook 2K for email. No problems, no bullsh*t and it's always on||||reg||1
Z||000023||Songman||05-25-2001||12:02 AM||||I thought the @Home software had to be loaded for some reason... although I don't use it...<P>I use Outlook 2k and IE 5.x for mine and love it.... I also didn't know IE6 was out. What are your impressions of it?<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000024||sjp777||05-25-2001||01:03 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Songman:<BR><B>I thought the @Home software had to be loaded for some reason... although I don't use it...<P>I use Outlook 2k and IE 5.x for mine and love it.... I also didn't know IE6 was out. What are your impressions of it?<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>They might tell you @home is needed, but they're lying. I just kept telling the tech no until he got the message.<P>IE6 is out as a beta. It's got some cool new features like the contact bar and the personal bar, and you can now pull images off the web or print them from a small toolbar that pops up in the image itself when you put the mouse pointer on it. I haven't noticed any bugs, but it loads a bit slower than 5.5. I have a fast hard drive and a P3 800, so it's not a whole lot slower, but on an older PC it might be annoying. I don't think the final code version is coming out until WinXP is released in Oct, but the beta is on the MS site for free download<p>[This message has been edited by sjp777 (edited 05-25-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000025||Songman||05-25-2001||01:18 AM||||I think I'll go get it and give it a try.. Thanks.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000026||Songman||05-25-2001||01:59 AM||||Wow! Is it my imagination or does IE6 actually run faster?!? So far, so good!<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||14
Z||000027||thomas_l||05-25-2001||10:17 AM||||The only thing the @home software does special is register your machine's MAC address with the DHCP server. That is the way they identify paying customers on their networks. No pay = no IP = no Internet. The AT&T guy that did mine had a config application that did the MAC registration without installing anything. But he did say his was an old version (he had a burned CD not a mass produced one) and that the new disc would install a bunch of BS.<P>If for some reason your MAC address changes (i.e. new NIC, new PC, new MAC belongs to a router  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">) your provider's tech support can update the DHCP server to let you log in. If it's a new router don't tell them you have a router since most providers TOS have included multi-user connections as a reason to kill your account. But then most new routers on the market will spoof the MAC addy from whatever device was used to turn the service on.||||reg||1
Z||000028||Will||05-25-2001||07:29 PM|||| <A HREF="http://www.aolsucks.com" TARGET=_blank>www.aolsucks.com</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000029||mblackwell||05-25-2001||08:51 PM||||For the record, I have used AOL.  Some of my family and freinds still do.||||reg||1
Z||000030||fierospeeder||05-26-2001||12:23 AM||||I have had about 5-6 years of experience using aol.  I had several accounts plus i used other peoples accounts, which i didn't have to pay for the service.<P>Aol has gotten worse in some ways.<BR>Im using aol right now, i have 2 months free service.  Trial membership because free juno cut its hours, so im going to be on juno 15 bucks or some other isp provider.<P>I called aol to cancel my free service, had some freaking airhead lady.  I asked if they charged my c/c and she said they did, i said can they remove the charges because im not going to use the next month, she says aol cant do it.  So she will give me next month for free because i wasn't satisfied with the service.  But i said can see do the free month then, then she said she can't.....<BR>She asked what was my dissatisfactions with aol.<P>1.  Aol connects at a really slow speed.  I get about 36, i use to get 46 when i was getting aol 4 free back then.  She asked what was i connecting, i told her i just know its 36 and aol 6 doesn't show the connection speed, but the older versions use too.  Shes saying thats odd.  She also tells me the phone lines only can handle 53k and most phone lines only give out 28k.  dont ask..    I told her, i get kicked off numerous times,  (aols kick off plan because your using their servers without doing anything) plus busy servers on occasion.  She tells me she read an article where aol as spent a lot of money making it the best #1 dial up connection.  blah blah.<BR>i told her i use to get better service with juno and was getting 46k with that.  <P>2. I told her aol would crash on me, she asked me if i remember what it said, i told her i dont remember.  But it is an aol error not a windows error.  I hardly get a windows crash.  she asks what ram do i have, 128 megs.  oh i guess thats enough and it cant be a memory prob.  Do you want to call our tech, uh no cause i have a ba in computer science and i know what im doing, (really im about a year away)  She goes on talking for another 10 minutes about how great aol is, and sends me something with her email address.  She also talks about how her kids didn't go to preschool for a month and she has to pay for it because her kids didn't go, and how that is related to my situation. i said ya, but i was on a free trial, i didn't pay anything yet.....<BR>I asked for the number for billing so i can try it, she gives it to me.<P>ring ring, hi im dwayne, hi i was wondering if you can take my charges off, ..... there are no charges on your c/c is my account canceled.  no its not.  F**%K b(t  i want my aol canceled.  he redirects my call and i talk to this young female.  She says well its billing time and what we can do is give you another free month and you can cancel it next month.   Thank you.  Bye, total time 1 minute.<P>Aol costs to much, and why are they charging me,  They have more advertisements then juno does.  I see more ads for a 20 dollar service then a free one.   Email is their only good thing and thats it.  Eventhough it can be hacked easily and so can anything else, thats why its the easiest.  Passwords are easily stolen on aol, and chatrooms are easily to freeze.  I know people who have done these things.<P>Ive been harassed on aol, and aol doesn't do anything about it.  Not sure what they are doing now, but for years they couldn't control the situation.  I had probably 10 people complain, numerous emails, numerous calls to their 800 number.  Some other people were harassed by the same individual.  A lot of the hosts/guides of the chatrooms knew what was going on and they knew the individual off hand.  But aol is a joke when it comes to this.  <P>aol is too highpriced, connection speeds suck, and they are offering dsl.  To much advertisments, lack of support in harassment, getting kicked off just like aol was 5 years ago.  <P>If your new to computers, go with aol for a few months then sign onto a isp.  Stay away from chatrooms, there are scary people in there, there are so many child molestors online and any individual can have aol, its like giving the keys to a drunk.  <P>||||reg||1
Z||000031||LZeitgeist||05-26-2001||02:20 AM||||Y'know, fierospeeder, I don't doubt you had those problems, but I don't see a single one that would have been limited to AOL usage... looks to me like any one or all of those problems could have come from any ISP you happened to call and complain about...||||reg||1
Z||000032||LarryB||05-26-2001||12:58 PM||||As far as the abusive behavior though, AOL is IMO about the worst.<P>In a previous life I found several providers that were completely disinterested in enforcing their own AUP against abusers.<P>Demon  (in England)<BR>Sympatico (in Canada)<P>And, of course, AOL.  They were the worst of the whole bunch.<P>2 other sites that were notorious were SUNY Stonybrook and WebTV, although both eventually saw the light and cracked down. <P>Most other providers would at least make themselves accessible and pay lip service as long as I provided them with the timestamped logs showing the unacceptable use.<P>The only time I ever knew AOL to do a dammed thing was when one of their chronic abusers finally publicly threatened in a chat room on one of the websites I was responsible for to carve up a woman with a knife, stuff the pieces into Baggies, and send them to her family.   Even then I had to call some VP who was listed as the administrative contact for the aol.com domain.  After that incident AOL changed all their contacts to role accounts. <P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by LZeitgeist:<BR><B>Y'know, fierospeeder, I don't doubt you had those problems, but I don't see a single one that would have been limited to AOL usage... looks to me like any one or all of those problems could have come from any ISP you happened to call and complain about...</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
Z||000033||thomas_l||05-27-2001||07:50 AM||||Actually, most ISP's do not host any real-time chat like AOL, making them a popular target for bad people. Add to that the number of completely naive users and not enough staff to really do any effective monitoring and the complaints are not a surprise. You're lucky if your regular ISP gives you a list of IRC servers and a how-to telling you what to do with them. And if you think AOL is unmoderated, you oughta try IRC. If you don't know what IRC is then stick to AOL. But then most people would use either web-based chat or some sort of instant messenger app.||||reg||1
Z||000034||LarryB||05-27-2001||10:36 AM||||It's not so much a question of channel moderation as the refusal of the providers to enforce their own Acceptable Use Policies, even when given proof of repeated gross violations.<P>With IRC if a channel op or sysop isn't around you can just /ignore &lt;nick&gt; all or /ignore &lt;user@host&gt; all, if everybody does it the pinhead usually gives up and moves on elsewhere since he's lost his audience.  <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000035||ray b||05-27-2001||11:51 AM||||AOL crashed twice today and froze my ibm i missed the start of monty c boo hiss<BR>never ever see above 33k with a 56k m some times 14k like 3 times today that sucks but down loads at 2or3k <BR>must refresh 4to6 times to see new post or my post on this and other boards<BR>adds adds and more adds<BR>wife and kids want to keep it for email address or i would dump it last year.<P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||1
